## 4. Controversy and Counter-Intuitive Points

**Conflict 1: The Labor Arbitrage Myth**
> **Industry says:** Nearshore's value proposition is cost savings through labor arbitrage.
> **David Sandoval says:** "If you're only looking at how much is it going to cost me per hour, that's probably not going to be the best play for a pure nearshore team." The real value is now capability, AI-enabled workflow, and same-workday collaboration — cost is a secondary outcome, not the goal.
> Evidence: "The whole value promise of nearshore was very much reduced to the cost structure... I believe now with AI that has provided a whole different set of opportunities."
> **Shareable potential: High** — directly challenges how most procurement teams evaluate nearshore.

**Conflict 2: AI Licenses Do Not Equal AI Value**
> **Industry says:** Distribute AI tools to your engineering team and productivity improves.
> **David Sandoval says:** "If you assume that just by giving out licenses, you're going to get the value... I don't think that's the case." You can blow the budget very quickly. The engineering framework and workflow layer matter more than the tools themselves.
> Evidence: "AI is going to sort of serve as a mirror to whatever already is happening in the culture of the team and their activities."
> **Shareable potential: High** — the "AI as mirror" framing is the most quotable, citable idea in the entire transcript.

**Conflict 3: Communication Barriers Are Not the Real Risk**
> **Industry says:** Language and communication are the primary risk of nearshore teams.
> **David Sandoval says:** The communication gap "is not as profound as some people may think." AI (meeting summarization, async context tools, real-time translation) has made this nearly irrelevant. The real risk is uneven AI adoption within a team — one person using AI aggressively while teammates aren't reviewing outputs — which introduces security vulnerabilities and quality degradation.
> Evidence: Sandoval on uneven adoption: "You could see them bringing in new practices into the team, but if they're not adopted by the rest of the team, then you're going to see uneven results — maybe bringing in some security risks as a result."
> **Shareable potential: Medium** — reframes the conversation from a people problem to a process problem.

**Conflict 4: AI Makes the Case FOR Nearshore, Not Against It**
> **Industry says:** AI will reduce demand for nearshore teams by automating what they do.
> **David Sandoval says:** AI doesn't flatten global delivery — it moves collaboration higher in the value chain. A well-run nearshore team with a strong AI operating model captures the benefits of AI AND the cultural/capacity advantages of nearshore. "Best of all worlds."
> Evidence: "If those teams are well versed in AI usage, you got a good working model, operating model, then you got a full benefit of all best of all worlds."
> **Shareable potential: Medium** — counterintuitive given the current AI-replaces-workers narrative.

**Conflict 5: Screen for Foundational Skills Before AI Proficiency**
> **Industry says:** Hire engineers who know how to use AI tools — that's the new baseline.
> **David Sandoval says:** "We want people to know how to use AI effectively, but they can do well if AI is not available." Screen for foundational engineering competency and cultural fit first. AI proficiency is third.
> Evidence: "The shift has been more going back to the basics. The basic skills of a programmer... those things need to be checked first."
> **Shareable potential: Medium** — runs counter to a lot of current hiring advice.

